ing, and shooting are the avatar-based examples."}, {"ch": 2, "topic": "Actions", "slide": 12, "q": "Can a game action exist without being attached to a player avatar?", "options": ["Only if the game is AAA", "Only during a cutscene", "Yes, for example build, swap, or rotate", "No, every action must move an avatar"], "answer": 2, "why": "The slides explicitly give non-avatar actions."}, {"ch": 2, "topic": "Actions", "slide": 13, "q": "In the action-platformer example, what makes run different from walk?", "options": ["Run pauses gameplay", "Run is walk but faster", "Run is a collision rather than an action", "Run always means firing"], "answer": 1, "why": "The lecture uses running as a faster movement verb."}, {"ch": 2, "topic": "Actions", "slide": 13, "q": "In the platformer, how can the player move horizontally while airborne?", "options": ["Only press the pause button", "Only use the build verb", "Only reach the exit first", "Combine jump with left or right movement"], "answer": 3, "why": "The slide labels jump/run for left or right movement."}, {"ch": 2, "topic": "Actions", "slide": 16, "q": "What is the recommended first step in designing actions?", "options": ["Write beta feedback first", "Set the market price", "Brainstorm verbs and define their types and scope", "Remove all inputs"], "answer": 2, "why": "Designing actions starts with brainstorming and defining verbs."}, {"ch": 2, "topic": "Actions", "slide": 16, "q": "Which is a design goal for the action list?", "options": ["Every verb has ten unrelated inputs", "As many verbs as possible regardless of purpose", "Enough verbs to avoid simplicity, but not so many that players get confused", "No way to reach objectives"], "answer": 2, "why": "The slides seek a balanced action list."}, {"ch": 2, "topic": "Actions", "slide": 16, "q": "What is verb bloat?", "options": ["Too many sound channels", "Too many player objectives", "Too much level scenery", "Too many verbs/actions cluttering the design"], "answer": 3, 